(window.guideContent=window.guideContent||[]).push(["frameworks",{"c:when-to-use-a-framework-and-when-not-to":"\u003cdiv class=\"co co-key\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">Key idea\u003c/p>\u003cp>A framework is worth adopting when it solves a need you actually have and would otherwise build yourself: durable state, human approval, multi-agent handoffs, tracing, sandboxed built-in tools, integrations or deployment. It costs you hidden prompts, version churn and some lock-in. Prototype the core loop in plain code, then adopt the smallest layer that meets a named need, and keep your prompts, tools and rules in your own modules.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>What plain code already covers\u003c/h4>\u003cp>The basic tool-calling loop is about 30 lines (Loop engineering module), and single calls, prompt chains, routers and parallel calls are a few lines each with a provider SDK. Anthropic's guidance to its customers is to start with the model APIs directly, because many patterns need only a few lines of code, and if you do adopt a framework, to understand what it does underneath, since wrong assumptions about the hidden layers are a common source of bugs.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Four kinds of \"framework\"\u003c/h4>\u003cp>The word covers very different products. LangChain's documentation offers a useful split, extended here with hosted options:\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"tw\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\u003cth>Kind\u003c/th>\u003cth>What it gives you\u003c/th>\u003cth>Examples (late 2026)\u003c/th>\u003cth>You still own\u003c/th>\u003c/tr>\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Framework\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Abstractions for models, tools and the agent loop; integrations\u003c/td>\u003ctd>OpenAI Agents SDK, Google ADK, LangChain \u003ccode>create_agent\u003c/code>, Pydantic AI, CrewAI, Vercel AI SDK\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Prompts, tools, evals, deployment\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Runtime\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Durable execution, persistence, streaming, human-in-the-loop\u003c/td>\u003ctd>LangGraph, Temporal, Inngest, Microsoft Agent Framework workflows\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Graph or workflow design\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Harness\u003c/td>\u003ctd>A ready-made agent: built-in file, shell and search tools, context compaction, subagents, permissions\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Claude Agent SDK, LangChain Deep Agents, Microsoft's Harness Agent\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Configuration, custom tools, sandbox\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Managed harness\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Someone else runs the loop, sandbox and session storage\u003c/td>\u003ctd>OpenAI Agents API, Claude Managed Agents, Managed Deep Agents, Google Cloud Agent Runtime\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Agent definition, tools, data and cost\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\u003cp>Many teams combine layers, for example a LangChain agent inside a LangGraph workflow traced in LangSmith, or Pydantic AI agents running under Temporal.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Needs that justify a framework\u003c/h4>\u003col>\u003cli>\u003cb>Durable state and resumability\u003c/b>: runs that survive restarts and continue days later.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Human-in-the-loop\u003c/b>: pause before a risky tool call, show it to a person, resume with their decision.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Multi-agent coordination\u003c/b>: handoffs, subagents, shared or isolated histories.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Tracing and evaluation\u003c/b>: every model and tool call captured with inputs, outputs, tokens and timing.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Built-in tools and sandboxes\u003c/b>: file editing, shell, browser, code execution, already hardened.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Integrations\u003c/b>: MCP servers, vector stores, hundreds of connectors.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Deployment\u003c/b>: servers, queues, streaming to UIs, scaling.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Team standardisation\u003c/b>: twenty engineers building agents the same way.\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003cp>Provider portability alone is a weak reason: a gateway (Reliability module) or an OpenAI-compatible endpoint gives you that without changing how you write agents.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>What it costs\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003cb>Hidden prompts and schemas.\u003c/b> Frameworks add system-prompt text, rewrite tool schemas and reformat messages. If you have never looked at the raw request, you do not know what the model sees.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Churn.\u003c/b> Agent frameworks change fast. LangGraph 1.0 deprecated its own \u003ccode>create_react_agent\u003c/code> in favour of LangChain's \u003ccode>create_agent\u003c/code>; in recent Pydantic AI releases \u003ccode>result.usage\u003c/code> is an attribute where older examples call it as a method. Pin versions and budget time for upgrades.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Lock-in.\u003c/b> Hosted runtimes store your sessions; vendor SDKs are tuned for their own models and tools.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Debugging through layers.\u003c/b> A failure may come from your code, the framework, the provider or their interaction.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Weight and attack surface.\u003c/b> Large dependency trees, more code paths that handle untrusted model output.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Keep the core yours\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Structure code as ports and adapters: prompts as versioned files, tools as plain typed functions, business rules and evals in your own package, and a thin adapter per framework. Then a framework change is a day's work, not a rewrite.\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode># atlas/tools.py: framework-free\nfrom pydantic import BaseModel\n\nclass TicketArgs(BaseModel):\n    title: str\n    priority: str          # \"P1\"..\"P4\"\n\ndef create_ticket(args: TicketArgs) -&gt; str:\n    \"\"\"Create a ticket in the tracker and return its id.\"\"\"\n    ...\n\n# adapters/openai_agents.py\nfrom agents import function_tool\n\n@function_tool\ndef create_ticket_tool(title: str, priority: str) -&gt; str:\n    \"\"\"Create a support ticket. Priority is P1 (urgent) to P4 (low).\"\"\"\n    return create_ticket(TicketArgs(title=title, priority=priority))\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>Before adopting a framework, run a one-day spike: build the same small agent in your two leading candidates, read the traces, and check five things: can you see the raw prompts, can you drop to the raw API for one step, does it support your providers, how often has it broken compatibility, and what licence and governance does it have.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Check yourself\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">List three needs that justify adopting an agent framework.\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Any three of: durable, resumable state for long runs; human approval with pause and resume; multi-agent handoffs or subagents; built-in tracing and evaluation; hardened built-in tools and sandboxes (files, shell, browser, code); ready-made integrations such as MCP and vector stores; deployment infrastructure (servers, queues, streaming); standardising how a large team builds agents.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">What is the difference between a runtime, a framework and a harness?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>A framework gives abstractions for models, tools and the agent loop (for example the OpenAI Agents SDK or Pydantic AI). A runtime gives durable execution, persistence, streaming and human-in-the-loop for workflows you design (for example LangGraph or Temporal). A harness is a ready-made agent with built-in tools, context management, subagents and permissions that you configure rather than build (for example the Claude Agent SDK or Deep Agents).\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">How do you keep the cost of switching frameworks low?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Keep prompts, tool implementations with their typed schemas, business rules and evals in your own framework-free modules, and write thin adapters that register them with a framework. Pin framework versions, and check that the framework lets you see raw requests and fall back to the provider API.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>","c:openai-agents-sdk-agents-handoffs-and-guardrails":"\u003cdiv class=\"co co-key\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">Key idea\u003c/p>\u003cp>The \u003cb>OpenAI Agents SDK\u003c/b> (Python and TypeScript, MIT licence) is a light framework with few primitives: \u003cb>agents\u003c/b> (instructions, tools, model), a \u003cb>runner\u003c/b> that executes the loop, \u003cb>handoffs\u003c/b> and \u003cb>agents as tools\u003c/b> for multi-agent work, \u003cb>guardrails\u003c/b>, \u003cb>sessions\u003c/b>, human \u003cb>approvals\u003c/b> and built-in \u003cb>tracing\u003c/b>. The \u003cb>Agents API\u003c/b> is the hosted alternative: OpenAI runs a managed Codex harness with a sandbox, context compaction and subagents, and you send tasks to a session.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>The primitives\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"tw\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\u003cth>Primitive\u003c/th>\u003cth>What it does\u003c/th>\u003c/tr>\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>\u003ccode>Agent\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Name, instructions, tools, handoffs, guardrails, optional \u003ccode>output_type\u003c/code> (a Pydantic model for structured output) and model\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>\u003ccode>Runner.run\u003c/code> / \u003ccode>run_sync\u003c/code> / \u003ccode>run_streamed\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Runs the loop until a final output; \u003ccode>max_turns\u003c/code> defaults to 10\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>\u003ccode>@function_tool\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Turns a typed, documented Python function into a tool with a generated JSON schema\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Handoffs\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Each target agent becomes a tool named \u003ccode>transfer_to_&lt;agent_name&gt;\u003c/code>; calling it passes control\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>\u003ccode>agent.as_tool()\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Calls another agent like a function; the caller keeps control (manager pattern)\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Guardrails\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Input, output and tool guardrails that can stop a run with a tripwire exception\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Sessions\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Conversation memory, for example \u003ccode>SQLiteSession(\"user-123\", \"chats.db\")\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>\u003ccode>needs_approval\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Pauses before a tool call until a person approves or rejects it\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Tracing\u003c/td>\u003ctd>On by default; every model call, tool call, handoff and guardrail is a span\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Two agents, a handoff and an input guardrail\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>import asyncio\nfrom agents import (Agent, Runner, function_tool, input_guardrail, GuardrailFunctionOutput,\n                    InputGuardrailTripwireTriggered, RunContextWrapper)\n\n@function_tool\ndef lookup_invoice(invoice_id: str) -&gt; str:\n    \"\"\"Return the amount and status of an invoice.\"\"\"\n    return f\"Invoice {invoice_id}: 120 EUR, paid\"\n\n@function_tool(needs_approval=True)\ndef issue_refund(invoice_id: str, amount_eur: float) -&gt; str:\n    \"\"\"Refund part or all of an invoice. Requires human approval.\"\"\"\n    return f\"Refunded {amount_eur} EUR on {invoice_id}\"\n\n@input_guardrail(run_in_parallel=False)          # blocking: runs before any model call\nasync def no_card_numbers(ctx: RunContextWrapper, agent: Agent, user_input) -&gt; GuardrailFunctionOutput:\n    digits = sum(ch.isdigit() for ch in str(user_input))\n    return GuardrailFunctionOutput(output_info={\"digits\": digits}, tripwire_triggered=digits &gt;= 13)\n\nbilling = Agent(name=\"Billing agent\", handoff_description=\"Invoices, payments and refunds\",\n                instructions=\"Resolve billing questions. Look up the invoice before refunding.\",\n                tools=[lookup_invoice, issue_refund])\ntech = Agent(name=\"Tech agent\", handoff_description=\"Bugs and error messages\",\n             instructions=\"Help with technical problems.\")\ntriage = Agent(name=\"Triage agent\", instructions=\"Route the user to the right specialist.\",\n               handoffs=[billing, tech], input_guardrails=[no_card_numbers])   # model= defaults to an OpenAI model\n\nasync def main():\n    try:\n        await Runner.run(triage, \"My card 4111 1111 1111 1111 was charged twice\")\n    except InputGuardrailTripwireTriggered:\n        print(\"Blocked: please do not send card numbers.\")\n\n    result = await Runner.run(triage, \"I was overcharged on INV-7, please refund 20 euros\")\n    if result.interruptions:                       # issue_refund is waiting for approval\n        state = result.to_state()\n        for item in result.interruptions:\n            print(\"Approve?\", item.tool_name, item.arguments)\n            state.approve(item)                    # or state.reject(item)\n        result = await Runner.run(triage, state)\n    print(result.last_agent.name, \"-&gt;\", result.final_output)\n\nasyncio.run(main())\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>What happens: the guardrail sees 16 digits and raises before any tokens are spent. In the second run the triage model calls \u003ccode>transfer_to_billing_agent\u003c/code>; the billing agent, which sees the conversation so far, calls \u003ccode>lookup_invoice\u003c/code>, then proposes \u003ccode>issue_refund\u003c/code>, which pauses the run. \u003ccode>result.to_state()\u003c/code> captures the paused run; it can be serialised (\u003ccode>state.to_string()\u003c/code>) and resumed later in another process. For tests, \u003ccode>agents.testing.ScriptedModel\u003c/code> replays scripted model outputs so you can check this flow without API calls.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Rules worth knowing\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003cb>Guardrail boundaries.\u003c/b> Input guardrails run only for the \u003ci>first\u003c/i> agent of a run, output guardrails only for the agent that produces the \u003ci>final\u003c/i> output. To check every call to a sensitive tool across handoffs, use tool guardrails.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Parallel versus blocking.\u003c/b> Input guardrails run in parallel with the agent by default (lower latency, but the agent may already have spent tokens or called tools when the tripwire fires). \u003ccode>run_in_parallel=False\u003c/code> makes them block first.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Handoff versus agent as tool.\u003c/b> A handoff transfers the conversation; the specialist answers the user. \u003ccode>as_tool()\u003c/code> keeps the caller in charge and returns the specialist's result to it. Use handoffs for routing conversations, agents as tools for a manager that combines results.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Other providers.\u003c/b> The SDK uses the Responses API for OpenAI models; other providers work through adapters such as LiteLLM or any OpenAI-compatible Chat Completions endpoint, with some hosted-tool features unavailable.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Tracing data.\u003c/b> Traces are sent to OpenAI's dashboard by default; disable or redirect them (custom trace processors) if your data policy requires it.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>The Agents API: the hosted option\u003c/h4>\u003cp>OpenAI's agent documentation offers three tiers:\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"tw\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\u003cth>\u003c/th>\u003cth>Agents API\u003c/th>\u003cth>Agents SDK\u003c/th>\u003cth>Responses API\u003c/th>\u003c/tr>\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Who runs the loop\u003c/td>\u003ctd>OpenAI's managed Codex harness\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Your application\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Your application\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>State\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Saved sessions, turns and items\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Your storage or SDK sessions\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Manual history or conversation objects\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Execution environment\u003c/td>\u003ctd>OpenAI-hosted sandbox, your own sandbox, or none\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Your runtime and sandbox providers\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Your own\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Effort to integrate\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Low\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Medium\u003c/td>\u003ctd>High\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\u003cp>With the Agents API (in beta as of late 2026) you create a session with an agent definition (model, instructions, tools including MCP servers and web search, optional subagents) and an environment, send it tasks, follow progress by streaming or webhooks, and steer it mid-task. It handles context compaction and resumption for you; you pay model, tool and container rates. Choose it for long-running tasks where you do not want to operate a sandbox; choose the SDK when you need control over storage, approvals and deployment.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Check yourself\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Build two agents with a handoff and an input guardrail: what do you write?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Define a specialist \u003ccode>Agent\u003c/code> (for example billing, with its tools and a \u003ccode>handoff_description\u003c/code>) and a triage \u003ccode>Agent\u003c/code> whose \u003ccode>handoffs=[billing, ...]\u003c/code> exposes \u003ccode>transfer_to_billing_agent\u003c/code> as a tool. Write an \u003ccode>@input_guardrail\u003c/code> function that inspects the user input and returns \u003ccode>GuardrailFunctionOutput(tripwire_triggered=...)\u003c/code>, attach it to the triage agent with \u003ccode>input_guardrails=[...]\u003c/code>, and run \u003ccode>Runner.run(triage, text)\u003c/code> inside \u003ccode>try/except InputGuardrailTripwireTriggered\u003c/code>. Optionally set \u003ccode>run_in_parallel=False\u003c/code> so the guardrail blocks before any model call.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">You attached an input guardrail to the billing agent, but it never runs when triage hands off to billing. Why?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Input guardrails run only for the first agent in a run, which is triage. Put the guardrail on the triage agent, or use tool guardrails on billing's tools if the check must happen at that point.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">When would you choose the Agents API over the Agents SDK?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>For long-running tasks where you want OpenAI to run the loop, the sandbox, compaction and session storage, and you accept less control and data held by the provider. Choose the SDK when you need your own storage, approval flows, deployment or non-OpenAI models.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>","c:claude-agent-sdk":"\u003cdiv class=\"co co-key\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">Key idea\u003c/p>\u003cp>The \u003cb>Claude Agent SDK\u003c/b> (Python and TypeScript) is Claude Code as a library: the same agent loop, built-in tools (read, write and edit files, search, run shell commands, fetch the web), context management, permissions, hooks, subagents and sessions. It is a \u003cb>harness\u003c/b>: you configure an agent rather than build one. \u003cb>Claude Managed Agents\u003c/b> is the hosted version, where Anthropic runs the harness and the session's sandbox for you.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Four ways to build with Claude\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"tw\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\u003cth>You want to\u003c/th>\u003cth>Use\u003c/th>\u003c/tr>\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Embed Claude Code's agent in your own Python or TypeScript process\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Agent SDK\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Work interactively in a terminal\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Claude Code CLI\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Call the model API and write the loop yourself\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Client SDK (Messages API)\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Have Anthropic host the agent loop and sandbox\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Managed Agents\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>An agent with file and shell tools\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>import asyncio\nfrom claude_agent_sdk import query, ClaudeAgentOptions, AssistantMessage, ResultMessage\n\noptions = ClaudeAgentOptions(\n    cwd=\"/srv/agent-workspaces/repo-123\",              # a throwaway checkout, not your laptop\n    allowed_tools=[\"Read\", \"Glob\", \"Grep\", \"Edit\", \"Bash\"],\n    disallowed_tools=[\"WebFetch\", \"Bash(rm *)\", \"Bash(git push *)\"],\n    permission_mode=\"acceptEdits\",                       # auto-approve file edits\n    sandbox={\"enabled\": True, \"failIfUnavailable\": True},   # OS-level isolation for Bash\n    max_turns=30,\n    max_budget_usd=2.00,\n    system_prompt=\"You are a careful engineer. Run the tests after every change.\",\n)\n\nasync def main():\n    async for message in query(prompt=\"Make the failing tests in tests/ pass.\", options=options):\n        if isinstance(message, AssistantMessage):\n            for block in message.content:\n                if hasattr(block, \"text\"):\n                    print(block.text)\n                elif hasattr(block, \"name\"):\n                    print(\"tool:\", block.name)\n        elif isinstance(message, ResultMessage):\n            print(message.subtype, message.num_turns, message.total_cost_usd)\n\nasyncio.run(main())\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>\u003ccode>query()\u003c/code> streams messages as the agent works and ends with a \u003ccode>ResultMessage\u003c/code> carrying the outcome, turn count and estimated cost. Underneath, the SDK runs a bundled Claude Code binary as a subprocess with the permissions of your process, which is why the workspace, permissions and sandbox matter.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Permissions: who decides whether a tool runs\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Every tool call goes through an evaluation order. The parts that matter most:\u003c/p>\u003col>\u003cli>\u003cb>Hooks\u003c/b> (\u003ccode>PreToolUse\u003c/code>) run first, on every call; a hook that denies a call wins even in the most permissive mode.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Deny rules\u003c/b> (\u003ccode>disallowed_tools\u003c/code> and settings files) are checked next. Naming a tool removes it from the model's view entirely; a pattern such as \u003ccode>Bash(rm *)\u003c/code> blocks matching commands in every mode.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>The permission mode\u003c/b> applies: \u003ccode>default\u003c/code> (unresolved calls go to your callback), \u003ccode>acceptEdits\u003c/code> (file edits approved automatically), \u003ccode>plan\u003c/code> (file-edit calls always go to your callback), \u003ccode>dontAsk\u003c/code> (never prompt; anything not pre-approved is denied), \u003ccode>bypassPermissions\u003c/code> (approve nearly everything except recognised destructive removals), plus newer modes such as \u003ccode>auto\u003c/code>.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Allow rules\u003c/b> (\u003ccode>allowed_tools\u003c/code>) approve matching calls.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Anything still undecided goes to your \u003cb>\u003ccode>can_use_tool\u003c/code> callback\u003c/b>.\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003cp>The trap: a call approved by an earlier step never reaches \u003ccode>can_use_tool\u003c/code>, so a check you put there is silently skipped for auto-approved tools. Put rules that must always apply in a \u003ccode>PreToolUse\u003c/code> hook or a deny rule. For a locked-down agent, combine an explicit \u003ccode>allowed_tools\u003c/code> list with \u003ccode>permission_mode=\"dontAsk\"\u003c/code>.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Sandboxing\u003c/h4>\u003cp>\u003ccode>sandbox={\"enabled\": True}\u003c/code> asks Claude Code to run shell commands under operating-system isolation (on Linux this needs \u003ccode>bubblewrap\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>socat\u003c/code>). In the Python SDK, if the sandbox cannot start, commands run \u003cb>unsandboxed with only a warning\u003c/b> unless you set \u003ccode>\"failIfUnavailable\": True\u003c/code>. Treat the SDK sandbox as one layer: in production also run the whole agent in a container or VM with only the workspace mounted, no credentials in the environment, and an egress allowlist (Security module).\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Extending the agent\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003cb>Custom tools\u003c/b>: decorate an async function with \u003ccode>@tool(name, description, schema)\u003c/code>, wrap it in \u003ccode>create_sdk_mcp_server(...)\u003c/code>, pass it in \u003ccode>mcp_servers\u003c/code>, and allow it as \u003ccode>mcp__&lt;server&gt;__&lt;tool&gt;\u003c/code>.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Subagents\u003c/b>: \u003ccode>agents={\"reviewer\": AgentDefinition(description=..., prompt=..., tools=[\"Read\", \"Grep\"])}\u003c/code> lets the main agent delegate to a specialist with its own context and tool set.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Sessions\u003c/b>: pass \u003ccode>resume=&lt;session_id&gt;\u003c/code> to continue a conversation, or fork it to try an alternative without losing the original.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Skills and memory\u003c/b>: skills, commands and \u003ccode>CLAUDE.md\u003c/code> files load from the project's \u003ccode>.claude/\u003c/code> folder, as in Claude Code.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Budgets\u003c/b>: \u003ccode>max_turns\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>max_budget_usd\u003c/code> stop runaway loops; the cost is a client-side estimate, so leave headroom.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Claude Managed Agents\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Managed Agents (beta as of late 2026) exposes the same idea as an API built on four concepts: an \u003cb>agent\u003c/b> (model, system prompt, tools, MCP servers, skills), an \u003cb>environment\u003c/b> (an Anthropic-managed cloud sandbox or a self-hosted sandbox on your infrastructure), a \u003cb>session\u003c/b> (a running agent instance doing a task) and \u003cb>events\u003c/b> (user turns, tool results, status updates). Anthropic runs the loop with built-in prompt caching and compaction, persists event history server-side, lets you steer or interrupt a running session, and supports permission policies, multi-agent orchestration and scheduled runs. Because sessions are stored server-side, Managed Agents was not eligible for zero data retention or HIPAA BAA coverage as of late 2026. Choose it for long-running, asynchronous tasks when you do not want to operate the agent process; choose the SDK when the agent must run inside your own process or network, or when data-retention rules require it.\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"co co-note\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">Note\u003c/p>\u003cp>The SDK's source is MIT-licensed, but its use is governed by Anthropic's Commercial Terms, and products built on it must use API-key authentication and their own branding rather than presenting themselves as Claude Code.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Check yourself\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Build an agent with file and shell tools in a sandbox: what configuration do you use?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Call \u003ccode>query()\u003c/code> with \u003ccode>ClaudeAgentOptions\u003c/code> that set \u003ccode>cwd\u003c/code> to a disposable workspace, \u003ccode>allowed_tools\u003c/code> to the needed built-ins (for example Read, Glob, Grep, Edit, Bash), \u003ccode>disallowed_tools\u003c/code> for dangerous patterns such as \u003ccode>Bash(rm *)\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>Bash(git push *)\u003c/code>, a permission mode such as \u003ccode>acceptEdits\u003c/code> (or \u003ccode>dontAsk\u003c/code> for a locked-down agent), \u003ccode>sandbox={\"enabled\": True, \"failIfUnavailable\": True}\u003c/code>, and limits via \u003ccode>max_turns\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>max_budget_usd\u003c/code>. In production, also run the process in a container or VM with no secrets and restricted network egress.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Your \u003ccode>can_use_tool\u003c/code> callback blocks writes to \u003ccode>.env\u003c/code>, but the agent edited \u003ccode>.env\u003c/code> anyway. Why?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>The agent ran in a mode or with allow rules that auto-approved file edits (for example \u003ccode>acceptEdits\u003c/code> or \u003ccode>Edit\u003c/code> in \u003ccode>allowed_tools\u003c/code>), and auto-approved calls never reach \u003ccode>can_use_tool\u003c/code>. Enforce the rule with a deny rule such as \u003ccode>Edit(./.env)\u003c/code> (or \u003ccode>Read(./.env)\u003c/code>, which also blocks edits) or a \u003ccode>PreToolUse\u003c/code> hook, which apply in every mode. Because file rules do not cover a script that opens the file itself, also enable the OS sandbox for real enforcement.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">When would you pick Managed Agents over the Agent SDK?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>When tasks are long-running and asynchronous and you want Anthropic to host the loop, sandbox, caching and compaction, so you only define the agent and send events. Pick the SDK when the agent must run inside your own process, network or infrastructure, or needs deep integration with your application code.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>","c:google-adk":"\u003cdiv class=\"co co-key\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">Key idea\u003c/p>\u003cp>\u003cb>ADK\u003c/b> (Agent Development Kit) is Google's open-source, code-first agent framework for Python, TypeScript, Go, Java and Kotlin (Apache 2.0). An \u003ccode>Agent\u003c/code> has a model, an instruction and tools; agents compose through \u003cb>sub-agents\u003c/b> (the model transfers control), \u003cb>agent tools\u003c/b> (one agent calls another like a function) and \u003cb>workflows\u003c/b> (graphs in ADK 2.x). It ships sessions, state, memory and artifacts, callbacks for guardrails, a developer UI, an eval command and deployment to Google Cloud. It is tuned for Gemini but runs other models through LiteLLM.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>A multi-tool agent\u003c/h4>\u003cp>\u003ccode>adk create my_agent\u003c/code> scaffolds a folder with \u003ccode>agent.py\u003c/code> and a \u003ccode>.env\u003c/code> for your key. The only required element is a variable called \u003ccode>root_agent\u003c/code>:\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode># my_agent/agent.py\nfrom typing import Any, Optional\nfrom google.adk.agents import Agent\nfrom google.adk.tools import ToolContext\nfrom google.adk.tools.base_tool import BaseTool\n\ndef get_weather(city: str) -&gt; dict:\n    \"\"\"Return the current weather report for a city.\"\"\"\n    if city.lower() == \"london\":\n        return {\"status\": \"success\", \"report\": \"Cloudy, 14 degrees C\"}\n    return {\"status\": \"error\", \"error_message\": f\"No weather data for {city}.\"}\n\ndef get_current_time(city: str) -&gt; dict:\n    \"\"\"Return the current local time in a city.\"\"\"\n    return {\"status\": \"success\", \"time\": \"10:30\"}\n\ndef block_unknown_cities(tool: BaseTool, args: dict[str, Any], tool_context: ToolContext) -&gt; Optional[dict]:\n    if args.get(\"city\", \"\").lower() not in {\"london\", \"paris\"}:\n        return {\"status\": \"error\", \"error_message\": \"City not supported.\"}   # tool is skipped\n    return None                                                           # tool runs\n\nroot_agent = Agent(\n    name=\"city_assistant\",\n    model=\"gemini-flash-latest\",\n    description=\"Answers questions about the time and weather in a city.\",\n    instruction=\"Use get_weather and get_current_time. If a tool returns status 'error', say so plainly.\",\n    tools=[get_weather, get_current_time],\n    before_tool_callback=block_unknown_cities,\n)\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>Run it with \u003ccode>adk run my_agent\u003c/code> in a terminal or \u003ccode>adk web\u003c/code> for the developer UI, which shows every event, tool call, state change and trace for a session. In your own code, a \u003ccode>Runner\u003c/code> (for example \u003ccode>InMemoryRunner(agent=root_agent, app_name=\"demo\")\u003c/code>) plus a session service drives the agent and yields events.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Points worth noticing:\u003c/p>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003cb>Tools are plain functions.\u003c/b> ADK builds the schema from type hints and the docstring, so the docstring is the tool description the model reads. Returning a dictionary with a \u003ccode>status\u003c/code> field gives the model an unambiguous success or error signal.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Callbacks are your guardrails.\u003c/b> \u003ccode>before_tool_callback\u003c/code> can inspect or rewrite arguments; returning a dictionary skips the tool and uses that dictionary as its result. Return \u003ccode>None\u003c/code> (not \u003ccode>{}\u003c/code>) to let the tool run. There are matching callbacks before and after the agent, the model call and the tool.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>A tool can take a \u003ccode>tool_context\u003c/code> parameter\u003c/b> to read or write session state, for example to remember the user's preferred city.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Composing agents\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"tw\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\u003cth>Mechanism\u003c/th>\u003cth>What happens\u003c/th>\u003cth>Use for\u003c/th>\u003c/tr>\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>\u003ccode>sub_agents=[...]\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\u003ctd>The parent model can transfer the conversation to a child, which then talks to the user\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Routing conversations to specialists\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>\u003ccode>AgentTool(agent=...)\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\u003ctd>The parent calls the child like a function and gets its result back\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Delegating a sub-task while staying in charge\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>\u003ccode>output_key=\"draft\"\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Saves an agent's final answer into session state; later instructions can reference \u003ccode>{draft}\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Passing results between steps\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>\u003ccode>Workflow(edges=[...])\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\u003ctd>A graph of agents, functions and tools with explicit routing (Graph engineering module)\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Deterministic multi-step processes\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\u003cp>The older template agents \u003ccode>SequentialAgent\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>ParallelAgent\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>LoopAgent\u003c/code> still run but are deprecated in favour of \u003ccode>Workflow\u003c/code> in recent 2.x releases, so prefer graphs for new code.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>State, memory and artifacts\u003c/h4>\u003cp>A \u003cb>session\u003c/b> holds one conversation's events and a \u003ccode>state\u003c/code> dictionary (short-term memory). A \u003cb>memory service\u003c/b> stores knowledge across sessions; an \u003cb>artifact service\u003c/b> stores files such as generated PDFs or uploaded images. Each has an in-memory implementation for development and persistent ones (databases, Google Cloud services) for production. ADK's documentation also stresses context management: older turns can be summarised and artifacts loaded lazily so that only useful tokens reach the model.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Models, evaluation and deployment\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003cb>Models\u003c/b>: Gemini by name; other providers through \u003ccode>LiteLlm(model=\"openai/&lt;model&gt;\")\u003c/code> or local servers such as Ollama and vLLM.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Evaluation\u003c/b>: \u003ccode>adk eval\u003c/code> runs saved eval sets of conversations and checks both the final response and the trajectory of tool calls; the web UI can turn a live session into an eval case.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Deployment\u003c/b>: Cloud Run, GKE, or Google Cloud's managed Agent Runtime; agents can also be exposed to other agents over the A2A protocol (MCP module).\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>When to choose ADK\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Choose it when you build on Google Cloud and Gemini, want one framework across several languages, or want built-in evaluation and a developer UI. Be aware that it moves quickly (graph workflows arrived in 2.0 and template agents were deprecated soon after), so pin versions.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Check yourself\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Build a multi-tool agent with ADK: what do you write and how do you run it?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Scaffold a project with \u003ccode>adk create\u003c/code>, then in \u003ccode>agent.py\u003c/code> write each tool as a typed Python function with a clear docstring that returns a dictionary with a status field, and define \u003ccode>root_agent = Agent(name=..., model=..., description=..., instruction=..., tools=[tool_a, tool_b])\u003c/code> with an instruction saying when to use each tool and how to report errors. Put the API key in \u003ccode>.env\u003c/code>, then run \u003ccode>adk run my_agent\u003c/code> for a terminal chat or \u003ccode>adk web\u003c/code> to inspect events and tool calls; in code, use a \u003ccode>Runner\u003c/code> with a session service.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">What is the difference between listing an agent in \u003ccode>sub_agents\u003c/code> and wrapping it in \u003ccode>AgentTool\u003c/code>?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>With \u003ccode>sub_agents\u003c/code>, the parent can transfer the conversation to the child, which then responds to the user directly. With \u003ccode>AgentTool\u003c/code>, the parent calls the child like a tool, receives its result and stays in control of the conversation.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">How do you stop an ADK agent from calling a tool with arguments you do not allow?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Add a \u003ccode>before_tool_callback\u003c/code> that checks the arguments and returns a dictionary (for example an error result) to skip the tool, or \u003ccode>None\u003c/code> to let it run.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>","c:microsoft-agent-framework":"\u003cdiv class=\"co co-key\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">Key idea\u003c/p>\u003cp>\u003cb>Microsoft Agent Framework\u003c/b> is the open-source successor to Semantic Kernel and AutoGen, built by the same teams, for Python and .NET (Go in public preview), MIT licence. It has four areas: \u003cb>agents\u003c/b> (a chat client plus instructions and tools), the \u003cb>Harness Agent\u003c/b> (a batteries-included agent for long tasks), \u003cb>workflows\u003c/b> (graphs of executors connected by typed edges, plus a functional API in Python) and \u003cb>integrations\u003c/b>. Its workflows pass typed \u003cb>messages\u003c/b> along edges, where LangGraph shares one \u003cb>state\u003c/b> object.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>An agent\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>from typing import Annotated\nfrom pydantic import Field\nfrom agent_framework import Agent, tool\nfrom agent_framework.openai import OpenAIChatClient     # pip install agent-framework agent-framework-openai\n\ndef get_order(order_id: Annotated[str, Field(description=\"The order number, for example 42\")]) -&gt; str:\n    \"\"\"Look up the status of an order.\"\"\"\n    return \"shipped on 3 March\"\n\n@tool(approval_mode=\"always_require\")                   # the run returns an approval request first\ndef issue_refund(order_id: str, amount_eur: float) -&gt; str:\n    \"\"\"Refund an order.\"\"\"\n    return f\"Refunded {amount_eur} EUR on order {order_id}\"\n\nagent = Agent(\n    client=OpenAIChatClient(model=\"&lt;model&gt;\"),           # also Foundry, Azure OpenAI, Anthropic, Ollama and more\n    name=\"SupportAgent\",\n    instructions=\"Help customers with orders. Look up an order before refunding it.\",\n    tools=[get_order, issue_refund],\n)\n\nsession = agent.create_session()                         # multi-turn state\nresult = await agent.run(\"Where is order 42?\", session=session)\nprint(result)\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>Tools are typed functions whose \u003ccode>Annotated[..., Field(description=...)]\u003c/code> hints become the schema. Around the agent you get \u003cb>sessions\u003c/b> for conversation state, \u003cb>context providers\u003c/b> for memory, \u003cb>middleware\u003c/b> that intercepts agent runs and function calls (logging, policy checks, redaction) and OpenTelemetry-based telemetry. The framework does not load \u003ccode>.env\u003c/code> files automatically; call \u003ccode>load_dotenv()\u003c/code> yourself.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>A workflow\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Workflows are built from \u003cb>executors\u003c/b> (a class with \u003ccode>@handler\u003c/code> methods, or a function decorated with \u003ccode>@executor\u003c/code>) connected by \u003cb>edges\u003c/b>:\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>from typing_extensions import Never\nfrom agent_framework import WorkflowBuilder, WorkflowContext, executor\n\n@executor(id=\"classify\")\nasync def classify(text: str, ctx: WorkflowContext[str]) -&gt; None:\n    await ctx.send_message(text)                          # message travels along outgoing edges\n\n@executor(id=\"refund\")\nasync def refund(text: str, ctx: WorkflowContext[Never, str]) -&gt; None:\n    await ctx.yield_output(f\"refund path: {text}\")        # final workflow output\n\n@executor(id=\"answer\")\nasync def answer(text: str, ctx: WorkflowContext[Never, str]) -&gt; None:\n    await ctx.yield_output(f\"answer path: {text}\")\n\nworkflow = (WorkflowBuilder(start_executor=classify)\n            .add_edge(classify, refund, condition=lambda m: \"refund\" in m)\n            .add_edge(classify, answer, condition=lambda m: \"refund\" not in m)\n            .build())\nevents = await workflow.run(\"I want a refund for order 42\")\nprint(events.get_outputs())\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>Agents become nodes through agent executors, and a whole workflow can be wrapped back into an agent with \u003ccode>.as_agent()\u003c/code>. Prebuilt \u003cb>orchestrations\u003c/b> cover common multi-agent shapes: sequential, concurrent, handoff, group chat and Magentic (a manager agent that plans and delegates).\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Agent Framework workflows compared with LangGraph\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"tw\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\u003cth>Aspect\u003c/th>\u003cth>Microsoft Agent Framework\u003c/th>\u003cth>LangGraph\u003c/th>\u003c/tr>\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Unit of work\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Executor with typed handlers\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Node function (or agent, or subgraph)\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Data flow\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Typed messages sent along edges; handler types are checked when the graph is built\u003c/td>\u003ctd>One shared state object; nodes return partial updates merged by reducers\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Routing\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Edge conditions, switch-case edge groups\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Conditional edges, \u003ccode>Command(goto=...)\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Parallelism\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Fan-out and fan-in edge groups\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Several edges from a node, \u003ccode>Send\u003c/code>, list fan-in\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Execution model\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Super-steps\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Super-steps (Pregel-style)\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Shared data\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Optional shared state (\u003ccode>ctx.set_state\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>ctx.get_state\u003c/code>)\u003c/td>\u003ctd>The state is the shared data\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Human input\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Request-info executors, approval-required tools\u003c/td>\u003ctd>\u003ccode>interrupt()\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>Command(resume=...)\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Persistence\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Checkpoints at super-step boundaries\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Checkpointers at super-step boundaries\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Results\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Output and progress events (\u003ccode>yield_output\u003c/code>, event stream)\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Final state, plus streaming\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Multi-agent\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Built-in orchestrations\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Subgraphs, supervisor and handoff patterns\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Languages\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Python, .NET, Go (preview)\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Python, JavaScript and TypeScript\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\u003cp>The practical difference: Agent Framework is \u003cb>message-passing dataflow\u003c/b> with typed contracts, which catches wiring mistakes at build time and suits pipelines whose steps exchange distinct types. LangGraph is a \u003cb>shared-state machine\u003c/b>, which is simpler for conversational agents where every step reads and appends to the same history. Both checkpoint at the same boundaries and both pause for humans, so either can host a long-running approval workflow.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>The Harness Agent\u003c/h4>\u003cp>For long, multi-step tasks Microsoft also ships an opinionated \u003cb>Harness Agent\u003c/b> with planning and to-do tracking, context compaction, file access and memory, \"don't ask again\" tool approval and observability: the same idea as the Claude Agent SDK or Deep Agents, inside the Microsoft stack.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>When to choose it\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Choose Agent Framework for .NET teams, for Azure and Microsoft Foundry deployments, when migrating from Semantic Kernel or AutoGen (migration guides exist for both, and new work is directed to Agent Framework), or when you want typed workflows with enterprise middleware and telemetry. Its docs carry a useful rule: if you can write a function to handle the task, do that instead of using an agent.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Check yourself\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Compare Microsoft Agent Framework's workflow model with LangGraph's.\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Both run graphs in super-steps, support conditional routing, fan-out and fan-in, human input and checkpoints at super-step boundaries. The core difference is data flow: Agent Framework executors receive typed messages from incoming edges and send typed messages onward (with optional shared state), and handler types are validated when the workflow is built; LangGraph nodes all read one shared state object and return partial updates merged by per-key reducers. Agent Framework also ships prebuilt orchestrations (sequential, concurrent, handoff, group chat, Magentic) and supports .NET, while LangGraph covers Python and JavaScript.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Which earlier Microsoft projects does Agent Framework replace, and what did it take from each?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>It succeeds AutoGen and Semantic Kernel. It takes AutoGen's simple single- and multi-agent abstractions and Semantic Kernel's enterprise features (session-based state, type safety, filters or middleware, telemetry, broad model support), and adds graph-based workflows.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">How do you make an Agent Framework tool require human approval?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Declare it with \u003ccode>@tool(approval_mode=\"always_require\")\u003c/code>. When the model calls it, the agent run ends with a response containing an approval request instead of a final answer; your application shows it to a person and passes the decision back in the next run. Workflows and orchestrations surface the same requests so the whole workflow waits.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>","l:frameworks/langchain-agents-and-deep-agents":"\u003cdiv class=\"co co-key\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">Key idea\u003c/p>\u003cp>In LangChain 1.x an agent is built with \u003ccode>create_agent\u003c/code>: a model, tools, a system prompt and a list of \u003cb>middleware\u003c/b> that hooks into the loop (summarise history, ask for approval, cap calls, fall back to another model, redact PII). It runs on LangGraph, so every agent gets checkpointing, streaming and interrupts, and can be dropped into a larger graph as one node. \u003cb>Deep Agents\u003c/b> pre-assembles a heavier harness for long tasks: planning, a virtual filesystem, subagents and context offloading.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>How the LangChain products fit together\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"tw\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\u003cth>Product\u003c/th>\u003cth>Role\u003c/th>\u003cth>Use when\u003c/th>\u003c/tr>\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>LangChain (\u003ccode>create_agent\u003c/code>)\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Agent framework: models, tools, the loop, middleware, integrations\u003c/td>\u003ctd>You want a configurable agent quickly, with any provider\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>LangGraph\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Runtime: graphs, state, persistence, interrupts, streaming\u003c/td>\u003ctd>You need explicit workflows (Graph engineering module)\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Deep Agents (\u003ccode>create_deep_agent\u003c/code>)\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Harness for long, multi-step tasks\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Coding, research or operations agents that run for many steps\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>LangSmith\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Tracing, evaluation and deployment (Agent Server)\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Always, for observability; optionally to host agents\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>create_agent with middleware\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>from pydantic import BaseModel\nfrom langchain.agents import create_agent\nfrom langchain.agents.middleware import (HumanInTheLoopMiddleware, ModelCallLimitMiddleware,\n                                         SummarizationMiddleware)\nfrom langgraph.checkpoint.memory import InMemorySaver\nfrom langgraph.types import Command\n\ndef search_docs(query: str) -&gt; str:\n    \"\"\"Search the product documentation and return the best passages.\"\"\"\n    ...\n\ndef create_ticket(title: str, priority: str) -&gt; str:\n    \"\"\"Create a support ticket. Priority is P1 (urgent) to P4 (low).\"\"\"\n    ...\n\nclass Answer(BaseModel):\n    answer: str\n    sources: list[str]\n\nagent = create_agent(\n    model=\"openai:&lt;model&gt;\",                        # \"provider:model\", e.g. anthropic:, google_genai:, ollama:\n    tools=[search_docs, create_ticket],\n    system_prompt=\"Answer from the documentation and cite sources. Open a ticket only if asked.\",\n    response_format=Answer,\n    checkpointer=InMemorySaver(),                  # use Postgres in production\n    middleware=[\n        SummarizationMiddleware(model=\"openai:&lt;small-model&gt;\", trigger=(\"tokens\", 4000), keep=(\"messages\", 20)),\n        HumanInTheLoopMiddleware(interrupt_on={\n            \"create_ticket\": {\"allowed_decisions\": [\"approve\", \"edit\", \"reject\"]},\n            \"search_docs\": False,\n        }),\n        ModelCallLimitMiddleware(run_limit=10, exit_behavior=\"end\"),\n    ],\n)\n\ncfg = {\"configurable\": {\"thread_id\": \"user-7\"}}\nout = agent.invoke({\"messages\": [{\"role\": \"user\", \"content\": \"VPN drops hourly, open a ticket\"}]}, cfg)\nif \"__interrupt__\" in out:                         # create_ticket is waiting for a decision\n    out = agent.invoke(Command(resume={\"decisions\": [{\"type\": \"approve\"}]}), cfg)\nprint(out[\"structured_response\"])\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>Middleware runs at fixed points in the loop: \u003ccode>before_agent\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>before_model\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>wrap_model_call\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>after_model\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>wrap_tool_call\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>after_agent\u003c/code>. Built-in middleware covers summarisation, human-in-the-loop, model and tool call limits, tool and model retries, model fallback, PII detection, a to-do list, LLM-based tool selection, context editing, shell and filesystem access, and subagents. You write your own with decorators such as \u003ccode>@before_model\u003c/code> or \u003ccode>@wrap_model_call\u003c/code>, for example to swap the system prompt and tool list depending on a \u003ccode>current_step\u003c/code> key in state (the handoffs pattern from the Graph engineering module).\u003c/p>\u003cp>\u003ccode>create_agent\u003c/code> replaced the older \u003ccode>create_react_agent\u003c/code> from \u003ccode>langgraph.prebuilt\u003c/code>, which LangGraph 1.0 deprecated; older tutorials still use it.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Deep Agents\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>from deepagents import create_deep_agent\n\nagent = create_deep_agent(\n    model=\"anthropic:&lt;model&gt;\",\n    tools=[search_docs],\n    system_prompt=\"You are a research assistant. Write findings to files and cite sources.\",\n    interrupt_on={\"edit_file\": True},              # pause before every file edit\n)\nagent.invoke({\"messages\": [{\"role\": \"user\", \"content\": \"Compare our three VPN vendors\"}]})\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>On top of \u003ccode>create_agent\u003c/code>, the Deep Agents harness adds:\u003c/p>\u003cul>\u003cli>a \u003cb>virtual filesystem\u003c/b> (\u003ccode>ls\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>read_file\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>write_file\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>edit_file\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>glob\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>grep\u003c/code>, and \u003ccode>execute\u003c/code> with sandbox backends) backed by pluggable storage: graph state, local disk, a LangGraph store or a composite;\u003c/li>\u003cli>a \u003ccode>task\u003c/code> tool that spawns \u003cb>subagents\u003c/b> with isolated context for long or parallel sub-tasks;\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>context management\u003c/b>: summarisation, offloading large tool results to files, and prompt caching;\u003c/li>\u003cli>optional planning with a \u003ccode>write_todos\u003c/code> tool, skills, long-term memory and human approval.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp>It can be self-hosted with the open-source SDK or run as \u003cb>Managed Deep Agents\u003c/b> on LangSmith. Compared with the Claude Agent SDK it is model-agnostic and built on LangGraph; the Claude Agent SDK runs Claude Code itself and is built for Claude models.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>In practice\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>Turn on LangSmith tracing (\u003ccode>LANGSMITH_TRACING=true\u003c/code> plus an API key) from the first run; it shows the exact messages middleware produced. It works for code outside LangChain too.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Pin \u003ccode>langchain\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>langgraph\u003c/code> and provider packages together; the 1.x line is stable at the core, but middleware and streaming APIs still evolve.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Keep tools framework-free and register them in the agent; a plain function with type hints and a docstring is enough.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Check yourself\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">What does \u003ccode>create_agent\u003c/code> give you that a hand-written loop does not?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Provider-agnostic model and tool handling, structured output via \u003ccode>response_format\u003c/code>, a middleware system for summarisation, approvals, limits, retries, fallbacks and PII handling, and because it runs on LangGraph, checkpointing with thread ids, streaming and interrupt-based human-in-the-loop, plus tracing in LangSmith.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Which middleware would you add to require approval before \u003ccode>delete_record\u003c/code> and to stop runaway loops?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>\u003ccode>HumanInTheLoopMiddleware(interrupt_on={\"delete_record\": {\"allowed_decisions\": [\"approve\", \"edit\", \"reject\"]}})\u003c/code> together with a checkpointer and a thread id, and \u003ccode>ModelCallLimitMiddleware\u003c/code> (and optionally \u003ccode>ToolCallLimitMiddleware\u003c/code>) with a run limit.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">When would you choose Deep Agents over a plain \u003ccode>create_agent\u003c/code> agent?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>For long, multi-step tasks such as research, coding or operations, where the agent benefits from planning, a filesystem to offload context, subagents with isolated context and automatic summarisation. For short, focused tasks a plain \u003ccode>create_agent\u003c/code> with a few tools is simpler and cheaper.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>","c:pydantic-ai-typed-agents":"\u003cdiv class=\"co co-key\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">Key idea\u003c/p>\u003cp>\u003cb>Pydantic AI\u003c/b> (Python, MIT licence, from the team behind Pydantic) treats an agent like a typed function: you declare the type of its \u003cb>dependencies\u003c/b> (database clients, the current user) and the type of its \u003cb>output\u003c/b> (a Pydantic model), and the framework validates tool arguments and the final answer against those types, asking the model to retry when validation fails. It feels like FastAPI for agents: dependency injection, type checking in your editor, easy testing.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>A typed agent\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>from dataclasses import dataclass\nfrom pydantic import BaseModel\nfrom pydantic_ai import Agent, RunContext, ModelRetry\nfrom pydantic_ai.usage import UsageLimits\n\n@dataclass\nclass Deps:                      # everything the agent's tools need, injected per run\n    customer_id: str\n    db: OrdersDB\n\nclass Reply(BaseModel):          # the only shape the run may return\n    answer: str\n    escalate: bool\n\nagent = Agent(\n    \"openai:&lt;model&gt;\",            # or \"anthropic:&lt;model&gt;\", or a model object for any OpenAI-compatible server\n    deps_type=Deps,\n    output_type=Reply,\n    instructions=\"You are a support agent. Use tools to look up orders before answering.\",\n)\n\n@agent.instructions              # dynamic instructions, computed per run\ndef customer_note(ctx: RunContext[Deps]) -&gt; str:\n    return f\"The customer id is {ctx.deps.customer_id}.\"\n\n@agent.tool\ndef order_status(ctx: RunContext[Deps], order_id: str) -&gt; str:\n    \"\"\"Look up the status of one of the customer's orders.\"\"\"\n    order = ctx.deps.db.get(ctx.deps.customer_id, order_id)\n    if order is None:\n        raise ModelRetry(f\"No order {order_id} for this customer; ask for a valid order number.\")\n    return order.status\n\nresult = agent.run_sync(\"Where is order 42?\",\n                        deps=Deps(customer_id=\"c-7\", db=db),\n                        usage_limits=UsageLimits(request_limit=5))\nprint(result.output.answer, result.output.escalate)   # result.output is a Reply\nprint(result.usage)                                     # requests, tokens and tool calls\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>What the framework does for you:\u003c/p>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003cb>Schema from signature.\u003c/b> The tool's parameters (after \u003ccode>ctx\u003c/code>) and its docstring become the JSON schema the model sees. Arguments are validated before your function runs; invalid ones go back to the model as an error to fix.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Typed output.\u003c/b> With \u003ccode>output_type=Reply\u003c/code>, the run only ends when the model produces something that validates as \u003ccode>Reply\u003c/code>. By default this uses a final \"output tool\"; native structured output and prompted modes are also available. \u003ccode>@agent.output_validator\u003c/code> functions can add business checks and raise \u003ccode>ModelRetry\u003c/code>.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>\u003ccode>ModelRetry\u003c/code>.\u003c/b> Raising it from a tool or validator sends your message back to the model and lets it try again, within a retry limit.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Dependency injection.\u003c/b> \u003ccode>ctx.deps\u003c/code> gives tools and instructions typed access to per-run resources without globals. Because \u003ccode>Agent\u003c/code> is generic over dependencies and output (\u003ccode>Agent[Deps, Reply]\u003c/code>), a type checker catches mismatches.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Limits.\u003c/b> \u003ccode>UsageLimits\u003c/code> caps requests, tool calls and tokens per run.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>\u003ccode>@agent.tool_plain\u003c/code>\u003c/b> registers a tool that does not need the context.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Testing without a model\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>from pydantic_ai import models\nfrom pydantic_ai.models.test import TestModel\n\nmodels.ALLOW_MODEL_REQUESTS = False          # any accidental real call fails the test\n\ndef test_reply_shape():\n    with agent.override(model=TestModel()):\n        result = agent.run_sync(\"Where is order 42?\", deps=Deps(\"c-7\", fake_db))\n    assert isinstance(result.output, Reply)\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>\u003ccode>TestModel\u003c/code> calls every tool with generated arguments and returns schema-valid output, which tests your wiring cheaply. \u003ccode>FunctionModel\u003c/code> lets you script exactly which tool calls and text the \"model\" returns, for testing specific paths.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Production features\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003cb>Human approval\u003c/b>: mark a tool \u003ccode>requires_approval=True\u003c/code> and include \u003ccode>DeferredToolRequests\u003c/code> in the output type; the run ends with the pending calls, and you continue it by passing \u003ccode>DeferredToolResults\u003c/code> with each call approved or denied.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Durable execution\u003c/b>: attach a durability capability and the agent runs under Temporal, DBOS, Prefect or other engines, with each model and tool call a durable step (Graph engineering module).\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Observability\u003c/b>: OpenTelemetry instrumentation; one line enables Pydantic Logfire, and any OTel backend works.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Evals\u003c/b>: Pydantic Evals for datasets, evaluators and checks on tool-call trajectories.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>More\u003c/b>: MCP client and server support, multi-agent delegation (an agent called from another agent's tool), and Pydantic Graph for state machines.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>When to choose it\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Choose Pydantic AI when your team writes typed Python, wants agents that look like ordinary well-tested application code, and needs provider freedom. It is lighter than LangChain, does not hide the loop, and its typed outputs and dependency injection make agents easy to unit-test. It moves quickly (for example, in recent releases \u003ccode>result.usage\u003c/code> is an attribute where older examples call it as a method), so pin the version.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Check yourself\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Build an agent with typed dependencies and outputs: what are the essential pieces?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>A dependencies type (for example a dataclass holding a database client and the customer id) passed as \u003ccode>deps_type\u003c/code>; a Pydantic model for the result passed as \u003ccode>output_type\u003c/code>; tools decorated with \u003ccode>@agent.tool\u003c/code> whose first parameter is \u003ccode>RunContext[Deps]\u003c/code> so they read \u003ccode>ctx.deps\u003c/code>; optional \u003ccode>@agent.instructions\u003c/code> that use the deps; and a run call \u003ccode>agent.run_sync(prompt, deps=Deps(...))\u003c/code> whose \u003ccode>result.output\u003c/code> is an instance of the output model. Validation failures in tool arguments or output are sent back to the model to retry.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">What does raising \u003ccode>ModelRetry\u003c/code> inside a tool do?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>It returns your message to the model as the tool's result with a request to try again, for example with a corrected argument, instead of crashing the run; the number of retries is limited.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">How do you test a Pydantic AI agent without paying for model calls?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Set \u003ccode>models.ALLOW_MODEL_REQUESTS = False\u003c/code> and run the agent inside \u003ccode>agent.override(model=TestModel())\u003c/code> (generated tool calls and valid output) or \u003ccode>agent.override(model=FunctionModel(fn))\u003c/code> (scripted responses), then assert on \u003ccode>result.output\u003c/code> and on what the tools did.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>","c:dspy-programming-instead-of-prompting":"\u003cdiv class=\"co co-key\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">Key idea\u003c/p>\u003cp>\u003cb>DSPy\u003c/b> (Stanford, MIT licence) lets you declare what each language-model step should do as a \u003cb>signature\u003c/b> (typed inputs and outputs plus a short instruction), choose a \u003cb>module\u003c/b> that decides how to prompt for it (\u003ccode>Predict\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>ChainOfThought\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>ReAct\u003c/code>), compose modules in ordinary Python, and then \u003cb>compile\u003c/b> the program: an optimiser tunes the instructions, the few-shot examples or even the weights to maximise your metric on your examples. You stop hand-editing prompt strings; you edit code, data and metrics.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Signatures and modules\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>import dspy\n\ndspy.configure(lm=dspy.LM(\"openai/&lt;model&gt;\"))        # LiteLLM-style ids: anthropic/..., ollama_chat/...\n\nclass Triage(dspy.Signature):\n    \"\"\"Classify a support ticket and decide whether it needs a human.\"\"\"\n    ticket: str = dspy.InputField()\n    category: str = dspy.OutputField(desc=\"one of: billing, bug, how-to, account\")\n    needs_human: bool = dspy.OutputField()\n\nclass TicketTriage(dspy.Module):\n    def __init__(self):\n        super().__init__()\n        self.classify = dspy.ChainOfThought(Triage)   # adds a reasoning field before the outputs\n\n    def forward(self, ticket: str):\n        return self.classify(ticket=ticket)\n\ntriage = TicketTriage()\npred = triage(ticket=\"I was charged twice this month\")\nprint(pred.category, pred.needs_human)              # typed fields, e.g. billing False\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>A signature can also be a string, \u003ccode>dspy.Predict(\"question -&gt; answer\")\u003c/code>. DSPy's \u003cb>adapters\u003c/b> turn signatures into actual prompts and parse the replies into typed fields; \u003ccode>dspy.inspect_history(n=1)\u003c/code> prints the last prompt and response so you can see exactly what was sent.\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"tw\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\u003cth>Module\u003c/th>\u003cth>What it does\u003c/th>\u003c/tr>\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>\u003ccode>Predict\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\u003ctd>One call that maps inputs to outputs\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>\u003ccode>ChainOfThought\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Adds a reasoning output before the answer\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>\u003ccode>ReAct\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\u003ctd>An agent loop: \u003ccode>dspy.ReAct(signature, tools=[fn, ...], max_iters=20)\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>\u003ccode>Refine\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>BestOfN\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Run several attempts and keep the best by a reward function\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>\u003ccode>CodeAct\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>ProgramOfThought\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Let the model write and run code\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Compiling against a metric\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>trainset = [\n    dspy.Example(ticket=\"I was charged twice\", category=\"billing\", needs_human=False).with_inputs(\"ticket\"),\n    dspy.Example(ticket=\"App crashes on login\", category=\"bug\", needs_human=True).with_inputs(\"ticket\"),\n    # ... 50 to 300 real, labelled examples\n]\n\ndef metric(example, pred, trace=None):\n    return example.category == pred.category and example.needs_human == pred.needs_human\n\noptimizer = dspy.MIPROv2(metric=metric, auto=\"light\")      # tunes instructions and few-shot demos\ncompiled = optimizer.compile(TicketTriage(), trainset=trainset)\ncompiled.save(\"triage_v1.json\")\n\nevaluate = dspy.Evaluate(devset=devset, metric=metric, num_threads=8)\nprint(evaluate(compiled))                                    # score on held-out examples\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>Every optimiser turns one or two of three knobs: the \u003cb>instructions\u003c/b> in each signature, the \u003cb>demonstrations\u003c/b> (few-shot examples) each module sees, and the model \u003cb>weights\u003c/b>. \u003ccode>BootstrapFewShot\u003c/code> runs your program on the training set and keeps traces that pass the metric as demos; \u003ccode>MIPROv2\u003c/code> searches over proposed instructions and demo sets; \u003ccode>GEPA\u003c/code> reads natural-language feedback from your metric and reflects on failed traces to rewrite instructions; \u003ccode>BootstrapFinetune\u003c/code> fine-tunes a model you can train. \u003ccode>compile\u003c/code> returns a new program and leaves the original untouched. Optimisers and their trade-offs are covered in depth in the Prompt engineering module.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>What the evidence says\u003c/h4>\u003cp>The original DSPy paper (2023) reported that compiled programs beat standard few-shot prompting by over 25% with GPT-3.5 and over 65% with Llama 2 13B chat on its tasks. The GEPA paper (2025) reported that reflective prompt evolution beat the reinforcement-learning method GRPO by 6% on average (up to 20%) with up to 35 times fewer rollouts, and beat MIPROv2 by over 10%. The DSPy docs also describe companies such as Shopify and Dropbox moving tasks to smaller models after optimisation. Treat these as reasons to try it, and measure on your own data.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>When DSPy fits\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003cb>Good fit\u003c/b>: a pipeline with a clear metric and at least a few dozen labelled examples (classification, extraction, routing, RAG answers, judges); frequent model changes, because you re-compile instead of re-prompting; cutting cost by moving a task to a smaller model.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Poor fit\u003c/b>: no metric or data; one-off prompts; products where legal or brand review must approve exact prompt text; open-ended agents where success is hard to score.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Mix and match\u003c/b>: DSPy programs are plain Python objects, so teams often use a compiled DSPy module as one node in a LangGraph graph or as a tool in another framework.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Check yourself\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Define a module with signatures and optimise it: what are the steps?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Configure a model with \u003ccode>dspy.configure(lm=dspy.LM(...))\u003c/code>; declare a \u003ccode>dspy.Signature\u003c/code> with typed \u003ccode>InputField\u003c/code>s, \u003ccode>OutputField\u003c/code>s and a docstring instruction; wrap it in a module such as \u003ccode>dspy.ChainOfThought\u003c/code> inside a \u003ccode>dspy.Module\u003c/code> subclass whose \u003ccode>forward\u003c/code> calls it; build a training set of \u003ccode>dspy.Example(...).with_inputs(...)\u003c/code> and a metric function \u003ccode>(example, pred, trace=None) -&gt; bool or float\u003c/code>; run an optimiser such as \u003ccode>dspy.MIPROv2(metric=metric, auto=\"light\").compile(program, trainset=trainset)\u003c/code> (or \u003ccode>BootstrapFewShot\u003c/code>, or \u003ccode>GEPA\u003c/code> with a feedback metric); evaluate the compiled program on a held-out set with \u003ccode>dspy.Evaluate\u003c/code>, and save it with \u003ccode>compiled.save(...)\u003c/code>.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">What can a DSPy optimiser change, and what stays fixed?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>It can change the instructions in each signature, the few-shot demonstrations each module uses, and (for fine-tuning optimisers) the model weights. Your program's structure, the signatures' fields and your metric stay as you wrote them.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Name one situation where DSPy is the wrong tool.\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>When you have no reliable metric or labelled examples, when the prompt text itself must be reviewed and fixed word for word, or for a one-off prompt where hand-writing is faster than building a dataset.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>","l:frameworks/smolagents-and-code-agents":"\u003cdiv class=\"co co-key\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">Key idea\u003c/p>\u003cp>A \u003cb>code agent\u003c/b> writes its actions as Python code instead of one JSON tool call at a time. One code action can call several tools, loop over results, keep intermediate values in variables and compute an answer, so tasks need fewer model turns. Hugging Face's \u003cb>smolagents\u003c/b> is a small library built around this idea (\u003ccode>CodeAgent\u003c/code>), with a classic \u003ccode>ToolCallingAgent\u003c/code> too. The catch is that you are executing model-written code, so it must run in a sandbox.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>JSON tool calls versus code actions\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Task: \"What is the mean price of KB-104, KB-105 and MS-220, and which is cheapest?\"\u003c/p>\u003cp>With JSON tool calling the model asks for \u003ccode>get_price\u003c/code> three times (in parallel at best), then needs another turn to compute the mean. As a code action it writes:\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>import statistics\nprices = {sku: get_price(sku) for sku in [\"KB-104\", \"KB-105\", \"MS-220\"]}\ncheapest = min(prices, key=prices.get)\nfinal_answer(f\"Mean {statistics.mean(prices.values()):.2f} USD; cheapest is {cheapest}\")\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>One step, exact arithmetic, and the tools compose naturally. The CodeAct paper (2024) evaluated 17 models and found code actions gave up to 20% higher success rates than JSON or text action formats. The same idea now appears across the industry: Anthropic and OpenAI both offer \u003cb>programmatic tool calling\u003c/b>, where the model writes code in a sandbox that calls your tools, and \"code execution with MCP\" applies it to MCP tools (MCP module).\u003c/p>\u003ch4>smolagents in code\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>from smolagents import CodeAgent, LiteLLMModel, tool\n\nPRICES = {\"KB-104\": 49.0, \"KB-105\": 59.0, \"MS-220\": 25.0}\n\n@tool\ndef get_price(sku: str) -&gt; float:\n    \"\"\"Return the current price of a product in USD.\n\n    Args:\n        sku: The product's stock-keeping unit, for example \"KB-104\".\n    \"\"\"\n    return PRICES[sku]\n\nmodel = LiteLLMModel(model_id=\"openai/&lt;model&gt;\")   # also InferenceClientModel, OpenAIServerModel for local servers\nagent = CodeAgent(\n    tools=[get_price],\n    model=model,\n    additional_authorized_imports=[\"statistics\"],\n    executor_type=\"docker\",                        # or \"e2b\", \"modal\", \"blaxel\"; \"local\" only for trusted input\n    max_steps=8,\n)\nprint(agent.run(\"What is the mean price of KB-104, KB-105 and MS-220, and which is cheapest?\"))\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>Each step the model writes its reasoning and a code block; the executor runs it; printed output and errors become the observation for the next step; calling \u003ccode>final_answer()\u003c/code> ends the run. \u003ccode>planning_interval\u003c/code> adds a periodic planning step, \u003ccode>managed_agents\u003c/code> lets one agent delegate to others, and \u003ccode>ToolCallingAgent\u003c/code> gives you classic JSON tool calls when you prefer them. The \u003ccode>@tool\u003c/code> decorator needs type hints and a docstring with an \u003ccode>Args:\u003c/code> section, which becomes the tool description.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Running model-written code safely\u003c/h4>\u003cp>By default smolagents executes code with its own \u003cb>LocalPythonExecutor\u003c/b>, which interprets the code's syntax tree operation by operation: imports are refused unless listed in \u003ccode>additional_authorized_imports\u003c/code> (submodules must be listed too), unknown operations raise errors, and the number of operations is capped to stop infinite loops. Trying \u003ccode>import os\u003c/code> fails with \"Import of os is not allowed\". That is a useful first layer but not an isolation boundary, and the docs say plainly that no local solution is 100% safe.\u003c/p>\u003cp>For anything that touches untrusted input (web pages, user files, other agents' output), run code in a real sandbox: \u003ccode>executor_type=\"e2b\"\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>\"modal\"\u003c/code> or \u003ccode>\"blaxel\"\u003c/code> (hosted sandboxes) or \u003ccode>\"docker\"\u003c/code> (your own container). Add the usual rules: no secrets in the sandbox environment, restricted network egress, CPU, memory and time limits, and a fresh sandbox per task. Prompt injection plus code execution is one of the most dangerous combinations in agent security (Security module).\u003c/p>\u003ch4>When code agents fit\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"tw\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\u003cth>Good fit\u003c/th>\u003cth>Poor fit\u003c/th>\u003c/tr>\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Data wrangling, maths, comparisons across many tool results\u003c/td>\u003ctd>A single lookup or a single action\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Tasks that chain several tools with loops or filters\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Environments where each action must be individually approved and audited\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Models that are strong at coding\u003c/td>\u003ctd>No sandbox available for untrusted input\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Check yourself\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Why do code agents often need fewer steps than JSON tool-calling agents?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>A single code action can call several tools, loop over items, store results in variables and compute derived values (such as a mean) in one model turn, whereas JSON tool calling needs a model turn for each round of calls plus another to combine the results, with arithmetic done by the model itself.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Is smolagents' LocalPythonExecutor a sandbox?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>No. It is a restricted interpreter that blocks unauthorised imports, unknown operations and runaway loops, which reduces risk, but it runs in your process and is not an isolation boundary. For untrusted input use a remote or containerised executor such as E2B, Modal, Blaxel or Docker.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Name two other places where the code-as-action idea appears.\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Programmatic tool calling from Anthropic and OpenAI (the model writes code in a sandbox that calls your tools), code execution with MCP, DSPy's \u003ccode>CodeAct\u003c/code> module, and coding agents that act through scripts and a shell.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>","l:frameworks/comparing-frameworks-a-decision-table":"\u003cdiv class=\"co co-key\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">Key idea\u003c/p>\u003cp>Choose on five axes: \u003cb>control\u003c/b> (how much of the loop and prompts you own), \u003cb>providers\u003c/b> (one vendor or many), \u003cb>tracing\u003c/b> (what you can see), \u003cb>durability\u003c/b> (what survives a crash or a long wait) and \u003cb>ecosystem\u003c/b> (language, cloud, community). No framework wins on all five. Most production stacks combine a thin agent framework, a runtime for long or approval-heavy flows, and a vendor-neutral observability tool.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>The decision table (late 2026)\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"tw\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\u003cth>Framework\u003c/th>\u003cth>Kind\u003c/th>\u003cth>Languages\u003c/th>\u003cth>Models\u003c/th>\u003cth>Multi-agent\u003c/th>\u003cth>Human approval\u003c/th>\u003cth>Durability\u003c/th>\u003cth>Tracing\u003c/th>\u003c/tr>\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Plain code and provider SDK\u003c/td>\u003ctd>None\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Any\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Any\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Your code\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Your code\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Your code\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Your code or OpenTelemetry\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>OpenAI Agents SDK\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Framework\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Python, TypeScript\u003c/td>\u003ctd>OpenAI first; others via adapters\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Handoffs, agents as tools\u003c/td>\u003ctd>\u003ccode>needs_approval\u003c/code>, resumable run state\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Sessions; Temporal integration\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Built in (OpenAI dashboard), custom processors\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>OpenAI Agents API\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Managed harness\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Any (HTTP)\u003c/td>\u003ctd>OpenAI\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Subagents\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Steering mid-task\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Hosted sessions\u003c/td>\u003ctd>OpenAI dashboard\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Claude Agent SDK\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Harness\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Python, TypeScript\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Claude\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Subagents\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Permission modes, hooks, callback\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Sessions with resume and fork\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Message stream, hooks, cost per run\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Claude Managed Agents\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Managed harness\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Any (HTTP)\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Claude\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Multi-agent orchestration\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Permission policies, steer or interrupt\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Hosted stateful sessions\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Session inspection and usage tracking\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Google ADK\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Framework with graphs\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Python, TypeScript, Go, Java, Kotlin\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Gemini first; others via LiteLLM\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Sub-agents, agent tools, workflows\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Human-input nodes, callbacks\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Session services, resume\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Dev UI, observability integrations\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Microsoft Agent Framework\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Framework and runtime\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Python, .NET, Go (preview)\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Many\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Orchestrations, workflows\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Approval-required tools, request info\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Workflow checkpoints\u003c/td>\u003ctd>OpenTelemetry\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>LangGraph\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Runtime\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Python, TypeScript\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Any\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Subgraphs, supervisor, handoffs\u003c/td>\u003ctd>\u003ccode>interrupt()\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Checkpointers, Agent Server\u003c/td>\u003ctd>LangSmith, OpenTelemetry\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>LangChain \u003ccode>create_agent\u003c/code>, Deep Agents\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Framework, harness\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Python, TypeScript\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Any\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Subagents, handoffs\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Middleware\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Via LangGraph\u003c/td>\u003ctd>LangSmith\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Pydantic AI\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Framework\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Python\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Any\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Delegation, graphs\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Deferred tools\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Temporal, DBOS, Prefect, Restate and others\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Logfire, OpenTelemetry\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>DSPy\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Programming and optimisation\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Python\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Any (via LiteLLM)\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Composed modules\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Not built in\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Not built in\u003c/td>\u003ctd>\u003ccode>inspect_history\u003c/code>, MLflow\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>smolagents\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Framework (code agents)\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Python\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Any\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Managed agents\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Step callbacks\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Not built in\u003c/td>\u003ctd>OpenTelemetry instrumentation\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\u003cp>Others you will meet: \u003cb>CrewAI\u003c/b> (role-based \"crews\" of agents plus event-driven \"flows\"), \u003cb>LlamaIndex\u003c/b> (retrieval-centred agents and event-driven workflows), \u003cb>AWS Strands Agents\u003c/b> (an open-source agent SDK and harness), and for TypeScript the \u003cb>Vercel AI SDK\u003c/b> and \u003cb>Mastra\u003c/b>. All the open-source frameworks in the table use permissive licences (MIT or Apache 2.0); the Claude Agent SDK's source is MIT-licensed but its use is governed by Anthropic's commercial terms.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Picking by situation\u003c/h4>\u003col>\u003cli>\u003cb>One or two model calls, or a fixed chain\u003c/b>: no framework. Use the provider SDK or an OpenAI-compatible client.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Committed to one vendor and want speed\u003c/b>: that vendor's SDK (OpenAI Agents SDK, Claude Agent SDK, ADK on Google Cloud, Agent Framework on Azure and .NET).\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>An agent that edits files, runs commands or browses\u003c/b>: a harness (Claude Agent SDK, Deep Agents, OpenAI's sandbox agents or Agents API, the Harness Agent), inside a sandbox.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Explicit workflows with approvals, long waits and several providers\u003c/b>: LangGraph (or ADK or Agent Framework graphs on those clouds), plus a durable engine for runs of hours or days.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Typed Python application code with strong tests\u003c/b>: Pydantic AI.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>A scored pipeline you want to optimise or move to a cheaper model\u003c/b>: DSPy, possibly inside one of the others.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Multi-tool data tasks where code is the natural action\u003c/b>: smolagents or a provider's programmatic tool calling, with a real sandbox.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>TypeScript product teams\u003c/b>: Vercel AI SDK, Mastra, LangGraph.js or the OpenAI Agents SDK for JavaScript.\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003ch4>How to run the decision\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003cb>Write the criteria first\u003c/b>: required providers, data residency, approval needs, expected run length, team language, hosting.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Spike two candidates for a day each\u003c/b> on the same small agent and the same 20 test inputs. Compare lines of code, what the traces show, how approvals and errors behave, and token usage. (The lab does exactly this.)\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Check the escape hatches\u003c/b>: can you see the raw requests, call the provider directly for one step, and export traces with OpenTelemetry?\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Check the project's health\u003c/b>: release cadence, how often it breaks compatibility, governance and licence.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Re-decide yearly.\u003c/b> This landscape changed every few months through 2025 and 2026; keeping your core framework-free makes re-deciding cheap.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Check yourself\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">A bank wants a mortgage-document assistant: Azure-hosted, .NET back end, several approval steps, runs that wait days for underwriters. What would you shortlist?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Microsoft Agent Framework first: .NET support, Azure and Foundry integration, typed graph workflows with checkpoints, approval-required tools and human-input requests. LangGraph (with a Python service) and a durable engine such as Temporal are the main alternatives. Whatever is chosen, approvals and long waits need durable checkpoints and a recovery process.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Which framework would you choose for a typed Python service that extracts invoice fields and must be unit-tested without model calls?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Pydantic AI: a Pydantic model as \u003ccode>output_type\u003c/code> gives validated fields, dependency injection keeps resources testable, and \u003ccode>TestModel\u003c/code> or \u003ccode>FunctionModel\u003c/code> with \u003ccode>ALLOW_MODEL_REQUESTS = False\u003c/code> lets you test without calling a model. (A plain provider SDK with structured outputs is also reasonable if there is no agent loop at all.)\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Name three checks you would make before adopting any framework.\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Any three of: you can see the raw prompts and tool schemas it sends; you can bypass it for a single step; it supports your required providers and hosting; it exports traces (ideally OpenTelemetry); its release history shows manageable breaking changes; its licence and governance are acceptable; it meets a named need you could not cheaply build yourself.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>","lab:frameworks/same-agent-two-sdks":"\u003cp>You will write the shop logic once, wrap it in two frameworks, run the same four requests through both and compare what each framework did for you.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Setup\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>Python 3.11 or later, \u003ccode>pip install openai-agents \"pydantic-ai-slim[openai]\"\u003c/code>\u003c/li>\u003cli>A model that supports tool calling. Either set \u003ccode>OPENAI_API_KEY\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>MODEL=&lt;model&gt;\u003c/code>, or point both agents at any OpenAI-compatible server with \u003ccode>BASE_URL\u003c/code> (for example OpenRouter or Ollama's \u003ccode>http://localhost:11434/v1\u003c/code>), \u003ccode>OPENAI_API_KEY\u003c/code> (any value for Ollama) and \u003ccode>MODEL\u003c/code>.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Steps\u003c/h4>\u003col>\u003cli>Create \u003ccode>shop.py\u003c/code>, the framework-free core shared by both agents:\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>ORDERS = {\"42\": {\"item\": \"headphones\", \"price\": 80.0, \"status\": \"delivered, damaged\"},\n          \"77\": {\"item\": \"keyboard\", \"price\": 45.0, \"status\": \"in transit\"}}\nREFUNDS: list[tuple[str, float]] = []\nINSTRUCTIONS = (\"You are a support agent for an electronics shop. Always look up an order before \"\n                \"discussing it. Refund only delivered orders and never more than the price. Be brief.\")\n\ndef lookup_order(order_id: str) -&gt; str:\n    o = ORDERS.get(order_id)\n    return f\"Order {order_id}: {o['item']}, {o['price']} EUR, {o['status']}\" if o else f\"No order {order_id}.\"\n\ndef issue_refund(order_id: str, amount: float) -&gt; str:\n    o = ORDERS.get(order_id)\n    if o is None or amount &gt; o[\"price\"]:\n        return \"Refund refused: unknown order or amount above the price.\"\n    REFUNDS.append((order_id, amount))\n    return f\"Refunded {amount:.2f} EUR on order {order_id}.\"\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003col start=\"2\">\u003cli>Create \u003ccode>agent_openai.py\u003c/code>:\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>import asyncio, os, sys\nfrom openai import AsyncOpenAI\nfrom agents import Agent, Runner, OpenAIChatCompletionsModel, function_tool, set_tracing_disabled\nimport shop\n\nset_tracing_disabled(True)   # remove this line to see the run in OpenAI's trace viewer\nmodel = OpenAIChatCompletionsModel(model=os.environ[\"MODEL\"],\n                                   openai_client=AsyncOpenAI(base_url=os.getenv(\"BASE_URL\")))\n\n@function_tool\ndef lookup_order(order_id: str) -&gt; str:\n    \"\"\"Return the item, price and status of an order.\"\"\"\n    return shop.lookup_order(order_id)\n\n@function_tool(needs_approval=True)\ndef issue_refund(order_id: str, amount: float) -&gt; str:\n    \"\"\"Refund an order. Requires human approval.\"\"\"\n    return shop.issue_refund(order_id, amount)\n\nagent = Agent(name=\"Support\", instructions=shop.INSTRUCTIONS, tools=[lookup_order, issue_refund], model=model)\n\nasync def main(text: str) -&gt; None:\n    result = await Runner.run(agent, text, max_turns=8)\n    while result.interruptions:\n        state = result.to_state()\n        for item in result.interruptions:\n            if input(f\"Approve {item.tool_name} {item.arguments}? [y/N] \").lower() == \"y\":\n                state.approve(item)\n            else:\n                state.reject(item)\n        result = await Runner.run(agent, state, max_turns=8)\n    print(result.final_output)\n    print(\"usage:\", result.context_wrapper.usage)\n\nif __name__ == \"__main__\":\n    asyncio.run(main(sys.argv[1]))\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003col start=\"3\">\u003cli>Create \u003ccode>agent_pydantic.py\u003c/code>:\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>import os, sys\nfrom pydantic_ai import Agent, DeferredToolRequests, DeferredToolResults\nfrom pydantic_ai.models.openai import OpenAIChatModel\nfrom pydantic_ai.providers.openai import OpenAIProvider\nimport shop\n\nmodel = OpenAIChatModel(os.environ[\"MODEL\"], provider=OpenAIProvider(base_url=os.getenv(\"BASE_URL\")))\nagent = Agent(model, instructions=shop.INSTRUCTIONS, output_type=[str, DeferredToolRequests])\n\n@agent.tool_plain\ndef lookup_order(order_id: str) -&gt; str:\n    \"\"\"Return the item, price and status of an order.\"\"\"\n    return shop.lookup_order(order_id)\n\n@agent.tool_plain(requires_approval=True)\ndef issue_refund(order_id: str, amount: float) -&gt; str:\n    \"\"\"Refund an order. Requires human approval.\"\"\"\n    return shop.issue_refund(order_id, amount)\n\ndef main(text: str) -&gt; None:\n    result = agent.run_sync(text)\n    while isinstance(result.output, DeferredToolRequests):\n        decisions = DeferredToolResults()\n        for call in result.output.approvals:\n            ok = input(f\"Approve {call.tool_name} {call.args}? [y/N] \").lower() == \"y\"\n            decisions.approvals[call.tool_call_id] = ok\n        result = agent.run_sync(message_history=result.all_messages(), deferred_tool_results=decisions)\n    print(result.output)\n    print(\"usage:\", result.usage)\n\nif __name__ == \"__main__\":\n    main(sys.argv[1])\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003col start=\"4\">\u003cli>Run the same four requests through both agents, answering the approval prompts as noted:\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003ccode>\"Where is order 42?\"\u003c/code> (no refund expected)\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ccode>\"Order 42 arrived damaged, please refund it\"\u003c/code> (approve with \u003ccode>y\u003c/code>)\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ccode>\"Please refund order 77\"\u003c/code> (the order is still in transit; if a refund is proposed, approve it to see what happens)\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ccode>\"Refund 500 euros on order 42\"\u003c/code> (approve with \u003ccode>y\u003c/code>; the tool itself should refuse)\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003c/li>\u003cli>For each run, record the final answer, the number of model requests and tokens, and whether an approval was requested. Note that the OpenAI SDK's usage accumulates across the paused and resumed run, while Pydantic AI's \u003ccode>result.usage\u003c/code> covers only the latest \u003ccode>run_sync\u003c/code> call, so add both calls together.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Compare the code: \u003ccode>wc -l agent_*.py\u003c/code>, then list what each framework generated for you (tool schemas, the loop, retries, approval plumbing) and what you had to write.\u003c/li>\u003cli>The refund policy \"only delivered orders\" lives only in the prompt. Move it into \u003ccode>shop.issue_refund\u003c/code> (refuse unless the status starts with \"delivered\"), rerun the third request in both agents, and confirm it is refused regardless of what the model or the approver does.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Optional, traces: remove \u003ccode>set_tracing_disabled(True)\u003c/code> and open the run in OpenAI's trace viewer; for Pydantic AI, \u003ccode>pip install logfire\u003c/code> and add \u003ccode>logfire.configure()\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>logfire.instrument_pydantic_ai()\u003c/code> at the top. Compare what each trace shows about the approval pause.\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003ch4>What you should see\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>Both agents call \u003ccode>lookup_order\u003c/code> before proposing a refund, and both pause before \u003ccode>issue_refund\u003c/code>. The OpenAI SDK shows the arguments as a JSON string, Pydantic AI as a dictionary.\u003c/li>\u003cli>The two agent files are of similar length (around 30 to 40 lines each); the shared \u003ccode>shop.py\u003c/code> is identical.\u003c/li>\u003cli>For order 77, many models decline because of the prompt, but some propose a refund anyway, and a hurried approver may click yes. After step 7 the refusal is guaranteed.\u003c/li>\u003cli>The 500-euro request is refused by the tool even after approval, because that rule was in code from the start.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Check yourself\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">What was the most important difference between the two SDKs in this lab?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>The approval and resume mechanics. The OpenAI SDK pauses with \u003ccode>result.interruptions\u003c/code>, and you approve on a serialisable \u003ccode>RunState\u003c/code> and resume with \u003ccode>Runner.run(agent, state)\u003c/code>, with usage accumulated across the pause. Pydantic AI ends the run with a \u003ccode>DeferredToolRequests\u003c/code> output, and you start a new run with the message history and \u003ccode>DeferredToolResults\u003c/code>, so usage must be summed across runs. Tool definition, the loop and schema generation were almost identical.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Why move the delivered-orders rule from the prompt into \u003ccode>shop.issue_refund\u003c/code>?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>A prompt instruction is a request the model usually follows, and an approver can make mistakes; a check in the tool's code is enforced on every call in every framework. Business rules that must hold belong in code, with the prompt explaining them so the model rarely hits the refusal.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>","b:one-agent-three-implementations":"\u003ch4>The brief\u003c/h4>\u003cp>This is a standalone build (you may reuse Atlas's search tools and corpus). Build a research assistant that answers a question by searching a corpus or the web, reading two to five sources and writing a 150 to 300 word answer with citations, within a budget. Implement it three times with the same tools, model, prompts and budgets: (A) plain Python with a provider SDK, (B) LangGraph, (C) one vendor SDK of your choice (OpenAI Agents SDK, Claude Agent SDK, Google ADK, Microsoft Agent Framework or Pydantic AI). Then measure them side by side and write the recommendation you would give your team.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Requirements\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>A framework-free \u003ccode>tools.py\u003c/code> (search, fetch or read a source, optionally save a note) and prompt files shared by all three implementations.\u003c/li>\u003cli>The same model, temperature and budgets everywhere: at most 12 model calls and a fixed token or cost ceiling per question, plus timeouts on every tool.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Citations: every claim cites a source id from the tool results, and a checker confirms each cited id was actually fetched.\u003c/li>\u003cli>An eval set of 20 questions with reference answers or grading notes, scored by a rubric-based LLM judge plus your own spot checks on at least five answers per implementation.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Tracing for each implementation, framework-native or OpenTelemetry, with one exported trace per implementation kept in the repository.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Failure injection: a tool timeout, a malformed tool result, a simulated 429 rate limit, and a retrieved document containing a prompt injection (\"ignore previous instructions and ...\").\u003c/li>\u003cli>The LangGraph version uses explicit state, parallel search with \u003ccode>Send\u003c/code>, a checkpointer and a citation-check cycle; the vendor-SDK version uses that SDK's native features (handoffs, subagents, sessions or guardrails) where natural.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Milestones\u003c/h4>\u003col>\u003cli>Write \u003ccode>tools.py\u003c/code>, the prompts, the 20-question eval set and the judge. Record a no-tools single-call baseline for reference.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Implementation A: the plain loop with budgets, retries, structured logs and citation checking.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Implementation B: LangGraph with a state table, plan, parallel search, read, write and a bounded citation-check cycle, compiled with a checkpointer.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Implementation C: the vendor SDK, using its idioms rather than imitating the plain loop.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Run the eval three times per implementation; collect quality, cost, latency (p50 and p95) and model calls per question.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Run the failure-injection suite and record what each implementation did: recovered, failed cleanly, or failed badly.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Measure lines of code (excluding the shared tools and prompts), number of dependencies and hours spent.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Write \u003ccode>COMPARISON.md\u003c/code>: a results table, three trace screenshots or exports, what each framework hid or revealed, and your recommendation.\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003ch4>Done when\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>One command per implementation (for example \u003ccode>make eval IMPL=plain\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>IMPL=graph\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>IMPL=sdk\u003c/code>) runs the 20 questions and writes a results file.\u003c/li>\u003cli>The comparison table shows quality, cost, latency, model calls, lines of code and failure-injection outcomes for all three.\u003c/li>\u003cli>All three enforce the same budgets and stop cleanly when a budget is reached.\u003c/li>\u003cli>The report says which implementation you would ship, why, and what it would take to switch to one of the others.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Stretch goals\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>Add a fourth implementation on a harness (Claude Agent SDK or Deep Agents) and compare autonomy against control.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Run implementation B or C under a durable engine (Temporal or DBOS) and kill the worker mid-run.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Swap in a small open-weight model and see which implementation degrades least.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Compile the answer-writing step with DSPy against your judge and plug the compiled module into all three.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Hints\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>Write the shared tools first and freeze their interfaces; most of the comparison's fairness depends on it.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Use the OpenTelemetry GenAI semantic conventions (or a single tracing backend such as Langfuse, Phoenix or LangSmith) so the three traces are comparable.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Expect quality differences to be small and differences in control, debugging and failure behaviour to be large; that is usually the real finding.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>"}]);
